      "raw_response": "Claim: Metastatic colorectal cancer treated with a single agent fluoropyrimidines resulted in similar efficacy and better quality of life when compared with oxaliplatin-based chemotherapy in elderly patients.\n\nANSWER: REFUTED\n\nSOURCE: The abstract from the FOCUS2 study\n\nMy confidence level for this claim is high because the study findings directly contradict the claim. According to the study, the addition of oxaliplatin did show some improvement in progression-free survival, but this was not statistically significant (p=0.07). Furthermore, the study found that the quality of life did not improve when fluorouracil was replaced with capecitabine, with 69 out of 124 patients reporting improvement in global quality of life with fluorouracil compared to 69 out of 123 with capecitabine. Additionally, the risk of having a grade 3 or worse toxic effect was not significantly increased with oxaliplatin, but was higher with capecitabine. This information suggests that oxaliplatin-based chemotherapy might have better efficacy and similar or even better quality of life outcomes compared to single-agent fluoropyrimidines in elderly patients with metastatic colorectal cancer.",
